WEBVTT

00:00.690 --> 00:05.790
A recipe for oatmeal cookies
calls for 2 cups of flour

00:05.790 --> 00:08.160
for every 3 cups of oatmeal.

00:08.160 --> 00:10.630
How much flour is
needed for a big batch

00:10.630 --> 00:12.592
of cookies that uses
9 cups of oatmeal?

00:12.592 --> 00:14.300
So let's think about
what they're saying.

00:14.300 --> 00:16.239
They're saying 2 cups of flour.

00:16.239 --> 00:25.345
So 2 cups of flour for
every 3 cups of oatmeal.

00:39.349 --> 00:40.890
And so they're
saying, how much flour

00:40.890 --> 00:42.570
is needed for a big
batch of cookies

00:42.570 --> 00:46.700
that uses 9 cups of oatmeal?

00:46.700 --> 00:48.200
Now we're going to
go to a situation

00:48.200 --> 00:50.580
where we are using
9 cups of oatmeal.

00:50.580 --> 00:56.187
Let me write it this
way-- 9 cups of oatmeal.

00:56.187 --> 00:58.020
And I'll show you a
couple of different ways

00:58.020 --> 00:58.769
to think about it.

00:58.769 --> 01:01.900
And whatever works
for you, that works.

01:01.900 --> 01:04.695
So one way to think about
it, so we're wondering.

01:04.695 --> 01:06.070
We're going to
say, look, we know

01:06.070 --> 01:09.130
if we have 3 cups of oatmeal,
we should use 2 cups of flour.

01:09.130 --> 01:12.170
But what we don't know is if
we have 9 cups of oatmeal,

01:12.170 --> 01:14.220
how many cups of
flour do we use?

01:14.220 --> 01:15.812
That's what they're asking us.

01:15.812 --> 01:17.520
But if we're going
from 3 cups of oatmeal

01:17.520 --> 01:21.330
to 9 cups of oatmeal, how much
more oatmeal are we using?

01:21.330 --> 01:25.530
Well, we're using three
times more oatmeal, Right?

01:25.530 --> 01:26.934
We're multiplying by 3.

01:26.934 --> 01:28.600
3 cups of oatmeal and
9 cups of oatmeal,

01:28.600 --> 01:30.700
we're using 3 times the oatmeal.

01:30.700 --> 01:33.070
Well, if we want to use
flour in the same proportion,

01:33.070 --> 01:35.650
we have to use 3
times the flour.

01:35.650 --> 01:39.190
So then we're also going to
multiply the flour times 3.

01:39.190 --> 01:40.940
We're going to multiply
the flour times 3,

01:40.940 --> 01:43.220
so we're going to have
to use 6 cups of flour.

01:48.920 --> 01:50.050
Ignore that question mark.

01:53.160 --> 01:54.550
And that answers the question.

01:54.550 --> 01:57.110
That's how much flour we need
for a big batch of cookies

01:57.110 --> 01:58.840
that uses 9 cups of oatmeal.

01:58.840 --> 02:02.040
The other thing is you
could set up a proportion.

02:02.040 --> 02:15.300
You could say 2 cups of
flour over 3 cups of oatmeal

02:15.300 --> 02:18.060
is equal to question mark.

02:18.060 --> 02:19.610
And instead of
writing question mark,

02:19.610 --> 02:21.030
I'll put a variable in there.

02:23.161 --> 02:24.910
Actually, let me put
a question mark there

02:24.910 --> 02:26.560
just so you really
understand it is

02:26.560 --> 02:29.530
equal to a question
mark in a box number

02:29.530 --> 02:37.615
cups of flour over
9 cups of oatmeal.

02:42.200 --> 02:44.047
And so I like this
first way we did it

02:44.047 --> 02:45.630
because it's really
just common sense.

02:45.630 --> 02:48.370
If we're tripling the
oatmeal, then we're

02:48.370 --> 02:50.440
going to have to
triple the flour

02:50.440 --> 02:52.820
to make the recipe in
the same proportion.

02:52.820 --> 02:55.030
Another way, once you set
up an equation like this,

02:55.030 --> 02:57.160
is actually to do a
little bit of algebra.

02:57.160 --> 02:59.140
Some people might call
it cross-multiplying,

02:59.140 --> 03:00.770
but that
cross-multiplying is still

03:00.770 --> 03:02.420
using a little bit of algebra.

03:02.420 --> 03:04.780
And I'll show you why they're
really the same thing.

03:04.780 --> 03:06.300
In cross-multiplication,
whenever

03:06.300 --> 03:08.160
you have a proportion
set up like this,

03:08.160 --> 03:10.920
people will multiply
the diagonals.

03:10.920 --> 03:14.030
So when you use
cross-multiplication,

03:14.030 --> 03:21.020
you'll say that 2 times 9
must be equal to question mark

03:21.020 --> 03:27.620
times 3, must be equal to
whatever is in this question

03:27.620 --> 03:31.420
mark, the number of
cups of flour times 3.

03:31.420 --> 03:36.750
Or we get 18 is
equal to whatever

03:36.750 --> 03:41.380
our question mark was times 3.

03:41.380 --> 03:44.640
So the number of cups of
flour we need to use times 3

03:44.640 --> 03:46.170
needs to be equal to 18.

03:46.170 --> 03:47.430
What times 3 is equal 18?

03:47.430 --> 03:49.180
You might be able to
do that in your head.

03:49.180 --> 03:50.030
That is 6.

03:50.030 --> 03:53.400
Or you could divide both sides
by 3, and you will get 6.

03:53.400 --> 03:56.770
So we get question
mark in a box needs

03:56.770 --> 03:59.070
to be equal to 6 cups of flour.

03:59.070 --> 04:01.575
Same answer we got through
kind of common sense.

04:01.575 --> 04:02.950
Now, you might be
wondering, hey,

04:02.950 --> 04:05.530
this cross-multiplying doesn't
make any intuitive sense.

04:05.530 --> 04:07.090
Why does that work?

04:07.090 --> 04:09.510
If I have something set up
like this proportion set up,

04:09.510 --> 04:12.490
why does it work that if I
take the denominator here

04:12.490 --> 04:14.490
and multiply it by the
numerator there that that

04:14.490 --> 04:15.906
needs to be equal
to the numerator

04:15.906 --> 04:17.560
here times the
denominator there?

04:17.560 --> 04:19.560
And that comes from
straight up algebra.

04:19.560 --> 04:22.760
And to do that, I'm just going
to rewrite this part as x just

04:22.760 --> 04:24.580
to simplify the
writing a little bit.

04:24.580 --> 04:28.350
So we have 2/3 is equal to--
instead of that question mark,

04:28.350 --> 04:31.110
I'll write x over 9.

04:31.110 --> 04:32.830
And in algebra,
all you're saying

04:32.830 --> 04:34.500
is that this
quantity over here is

04:34.500 --> 04:36.850
equal to this
quantity over here.

04:36.850 --> 04:38.840
So if you do anything
to what's on the left,

04:38.840 --> 04:40.310
if you want it to
still be equal,

04:40.310 --> 04:42.400
if the thing on the right
still needs to be equal,

04:42.400 --> 04:44.470
you have to do the
same thing to it.

04:44.470 --> 04:47.220
Now, what we want to do is we
want to simplify this so all

04:47.220 --> 04:49.530
we have on the
right-hand side is an x.

04:49.530 --> 04:52.309
So what can we
multiply this by so

04:52.309 --> 04:53.600
that we're just left with an x?

04:53.600 --> 04:55.520
So that we've solved for x?

04:55.520 --> 04:57.550
Well, if we multiply
this times 9,

04:57.550 --> 04:59.390
the 9's are going to cancel out.

04:59.390 --> 05:00.970
So let's multiply
the right by 9.

05:00.970 --> 05:02.844
But of course, if we
multiply the right by 9,

05:02.844 --> 05:04.850
we have to still
multiply the left by 9.

05:04.850 --> 05:06.740
Otherwise they still
wouldn't be equal.

05:06.740 --> 05:09.470
If they were equal before
being multiplied by 9, for them

05:09.470 --> 05:12.720
to still be equal, you have to
multiply 9 times both sides.

05:12.720 --> 05:14.760
On the right-hand side,
the 9's cancel out,

05:14.760 --> 05:16.330
so you're just left with an x.

05:16.330 --> 05:21.490
On the left-hand side, you have
9 times 2/3, or 9/1 times 2/3.

05:21.490 --> 05:24.770
Or this is equal to 18/3.

05:24.770 --> 05:28.300
And we know that 18/3
is the same thing as 6.

05:28.300 --> 05:30.440
So these are all
legitimate ways to do it.

05:30.440 --> 05:32.340
I wanted you to understand
that what I'm doing right here

05:32.340 --> 05:32.840
is algebra.

05:32.840 --> 05:35.840
That's actually the reasoning
why cross-multiplication works.

05:35.840 --> 05:37.950
But for a really simple
problem like this,

05:37.950 --> 05:39.740
you could really just
use common sense.

05:39.740 --> 05:43.520
If you're increasing the cups
of oatmeal by a factor of 3,

05:43.520 --> 05:46.799
then increase the cups of
flour by a factor of 3.
